{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"undecided-knitted-gaur","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# The 800-LOC cap is saturated: nine modules within 20 lines, three exactly at it\n\n## Measured (this worktree, rebased on current origin/main)\n\n| file | LOC | headroom |\n|---|---|---|\n| `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_differ.py` | **800** | **0** |\n| `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest.py` | **800** | **0** |\n| `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/passes.py` | **800** | **0** |\n| `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py` | 799 | 1 |\n| `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/orchestrator.py` | 796 | 4 |\n| `src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py` | 793 | 7 |\n| `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/applier.py` | 791 | 9 |\n| `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/workflow_ops.py` | 787 | 13 |\n| `src/rebar/_store/event_append.py` | 782 | 18 |\n\n## Why this is a blocker, not an observation\n\nIt is already blocking real work. Story 343b's remaining gap (the gate verdict must carry\n`provider_provenance`; see that ticket's \"gap 5\") requires threading one value through\n`orchestrator.finalize_verdict` and its FIVE call sites, which live in `orchestrator.py` (4 lines\nof headroom), `workflow_ops.py` (13) and `gate_dispatch.py` (**1 line**, with THREE of the five\ncall sites). The minimal possible change — one helper, one defaulted parameter, one added key, one\nargument per call site — cannot fit. Even folding the helper into a roomy module leaves\n`gate_dispatch.py` needing +3 lines against 1 line of headroom, and the kwarg cannot be appended to\nthe existing lines without breaching the 100-char E501 limit.\n\nSo a correct, minimal, well-tested change is physically unlandable until something is split.\n\n## This is the third instance in one epic\n\n- `runner.py` hit 800 during story dc0d -> task 2682 split it.\n- `runner.py` hit it AGAIN after main grew it to 798 -> ticket 3a98 split it further.\n- `attest.py` was at 799 and a two-call-site kwarg change only fit by importing a function directly\n  to dodge an E501 wrap, landing at EXACTLY 800 -> ticket c6c9 filed to split it, still open.\nEach was treated as a one-off. Nine modules at the edge says it is not.\n\n## The cost is not only structural\n\nThe cap is now shaping documentation and design quality, not just file layout:\n- In `attest.py` the explanatory comments for a security-relevant change had to be cut to a pointer\n  purely to save lines.\n- Here, the natural home for the cfg->record helper is beside its consumer; it has to go elsewhere\n  purely for space, which is the \"split mechanically to hit a number\" the module-size policy itself\n  forbids.\n- The next person to fix a one-line bug in any of the nine pays an unrelated refactor first, under\n  whatever time pressure that bug carries.\n\n## Approach (candidate, deliberately not a decision)\n\nDo NOT raise the limit — it is locked, and an administrator override is the documented path for a\nlimit change precisely so this stays deliberate. Instead, treat the saturated set as a queue and\nsplit along seams that ALREADY exist in the call graph, one module at a time, as 2682 and 3a98 did.\nBoth of those are worked precedents: 2682 extracted the structured-run cluster with ZERO test edits;\n3a98 moved the pai preflight cluster into it, also with zero test edits, by re-importing the moved\nnames so existing monkeypatch seams kept resolving.\n\nPriority should follow what is BLOCKING rather than what is largest: `gate_dispatch.py` (1 line,\nblocking 343b today), then `orchestrator.py` (4), then the three at exactly 800.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py` is under 700 LOC, split along an existing call-graph\n      seam, with zero test edits or each edit justified in the commit message\n- [ ] `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/orchestrator.py` is under 700 LOC on the same terms\n- [ ] no extracted module is under 100 LOC (the anti-fragmentation floor)\n- [ ] a test asserts the 343b gap-5 wiring lands: the plan-review gate verdict carries\n      `provider_provenance`, exercised through the real assembly path rather than a hand-built dict\n- [ ] [operator-attested] the full suite passes with a test count equal to the pre-split count plus\n      any new seam tests, evidenced by the two pytest summary lines in a comment","id":"1484-c197-0f71-471e","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Split the modules saturating the 800-LOC cap, starting with gate_dispatch"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785466172302361001,"uuid":"60174cc7-1b1e-452c-8311-acb76b80181a"}